# Kconfig.brain — Brain server link defaults and subsumption layer enables
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  Compile-time defaults for the brain link.
# Runtime values can be overridden in CONFIG.INI.

menu "Brain Server Link"

config BRAIN_L1_ENABLED
    bool "Behavior layer 1 (L1 — obstacle avoidance)"
    default y
    help
      Enables the L1 reactive obstacle-avoidance layer of the
      Subsumption architecture.  When active, proximity sensor readings
      override motor commands to avoid collisions.
      Compile-time default; runtime toggle via CONFIG.INI `behavior_l1`.

config BRAIN_L2_ENABLED
    bool "Behavior layer 2 (L2 — remote VLA brain link)"
    default y
    help
      Enables the L2 remote VLA (Vision-Language-Action) layer.
      The kernel connects to the Python brain server and dispatches
      camera frames / sensor packets for AI planning.
      Compile-time default; runtime toggle via CONFIG.INI `behavior_l2`.

config BRAIN_L3_ENABLED
    bool "Behavior layer 3 (L3 — autonomous exploration)"
    default y
    help
      Enables the L3 autonomous-exploration layer.  When neither L1
      nor L2 produce commands, L3 drives the robot to explore its
      environment using nav/occupancy grid waypoints.
      Compile-time default; runtime toggle via CONFIG.INI `behavior_l3`.

config AUTO_RECONNECT
    bool "Auto-reconnect to brain server on connection loss"
    default y
    help
      When the brain link drops, the kernel automatically retries
      the TCP connection every OFFLINE_RECONNECT_INTERVAL_TICKS ticks.
      Disable to stay in offline-autonomous mode permanently after
      first disconnect (e.g. for field deployments with no brain).

config MULTISTREAM_SCHED_PRIORITY
    bool "Multi-stream scheduler: priority (control preempts bulk)"
    default n
    help
      RFC-0021 multi-stream link scheduling policy — a compile-time
      choice so each binary carries exactly one policy (zero hot-path
      overhead; the unused branch is const-eliminated).

      n (default) = FIFO: streams share the link in send order; a large
        STREAM_CAMERA frame can head-of-line-block a STREAM_CONTROL frame.
      y = PRIORITY: STREAM_CONTROL frames are interleaved ahead of bulk
        stream chunks, bounding control hold-off to ~one chunk-time
        instead of one full bulk-frame-time (experiment I2).

      Selected via `make menuconfig` / defconfig; surfaces as
      `robot_os_limits::MULTISTREAM_SCHED_PRIORITY`. A/B the two builds
      with the bench_boot capture + dashboard.

endmenu # Brain Server Link
